<Docs
  title="Installing GitWarren — desktop app or command line"
  description="Install GitWarren as a desktop app on macOS, Windows or Linux, or run the command that serves the same review UI into a browser tab on any machine with Node."
  eyebrow="Installing"
  heading="The app, or the command."
  intro="GitWarren is a review UI with two shells around it. One is an Electron window with a tray icon; the other is a local daemon serving the same pages to a browser tab. Every line of the UI is the same in both — pick the shell, not the product."
>
  <h2>The desktop app</h2>

  <p>
    The app is what you want on a machine you sit at. It brings a tray icon so
    GitWarren is always running, a <code>gitwarren://</code> protocol handler so
    links from your terminal open the right review, and silent background
    updates.
  </p>

  <p>
    Download it from <a href="/#download">the front page</a>, or on macOS:
  </p>

  <pre><code>brew install --cask klarluft/tap/gitwarren</code></pre>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Platform</th><th>What you get</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>macOS</td>
          <td>
            A <code>.dmg</code> per architecture — Apple silicon and Intel are
            separate downloads. Signed and notarised, so it updates itself.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Windows</td>
          <td>
            One installer that carries both x64 and arm64. It installs per user,
            so there is no UAC prompt on install or on update. See <a
              href="/docs/windows">Running on Windows</a>.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Linux</td>
          <td>
            An AppImage, x86_64 or arm64. Deliberately not a deb or an rpm — see{" "}
            <a href="/docs/linux">Running on Linux</a> for why.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>The command line</h2>

  <p>
    The same GitWarren, with a browser tab for a shell and no Electron anywhere
    in it. It exists for two audiences the app cannot serve: someone who will
    not install an Electron app, and a machine with no screen at all — a VPS, a
    WSL distro, a box an agent works on.
  </p>

  <pre><code><span class="c"># brings its own Node — macOS and Linux</span>
brew install klarluft/tap/gitwarren-cli

<span class="c"># the same, without Homebrew — macOS and Linux</span>
curl -fsSL https://gitwarren.com/install.sh | sh

<span class="c"># uses the Node you already have (22 or newer) — anywhere, including Windows</span>
npx gitwarren serve</code></pre>

  <p>
    The formula is <code>gitwarren-cli</code> and the cask is <code
      >gitwarren</code
    >, so <code>brew install --cask klarluft/tap/gitwarren</code> keeps meaning
    the app. The binary is called <code>gitwarren</code> whichever way it
    arrived.
  </p>

  <p>
    The install script unpacks the release tarball into{" "}
    <code>~/.gitwarren/daemon/&lt;version&gt;/</code> and writes{" "}
    <code>~/.gitwarren/bin/gitwarren</code>, which you add to your{" "}
    <code>PATH</code>. That is the same layout the app produces when it installs
    GitWarren onto <a href="/docs/another-machine">another machine</a> over
    ssh, so either can upgrade what the other installed — and so can{" "}
    <code>gitwarren update</code>, which is this script from the inside.{" "}
    <code>gitwarren uninstall</code> takes the whole of it back off the machine.
    Your reviews are in neither place and are kept by both.
  </p>

  <h3>Which command you want</h3>

  <p>
    Four things a person wants from it, and one command for each. They are
    independent — none of them needs another to have been run first.
  </p>

  <pre><code><span class="c"># Run it now — in this terminal, until Ctrl-C</span>
gitwarren serve --open     <span class="c"># serve the UI on 127.0.0.1 and open it (or just print the URL, without --open)</span>
gitwarren open [link]      <span class="c"># open the running GitWarren in your browser, later</span>

<span class="c"># Keep it running — in the background, from now and at every login</span>
gitwarren service install
gitwarren service uninstall
gitwarren service status

<span class="c"># Let a coding agent in</span>
gitwarren agent-setup      <span class="c"># the one sentence to give an agent, see below</span>

<span class="c"># Keep it current, or take it off again</span>
gitwarren update           <span class="c"># move to the newest release (--check only says whether there is one)</span>
gitwarren doctor           <span class="c"># is every path GitWarren hands out still pointing at something?</span>
gitwarren uninstall        <span class="c"># remove it from this machine; --data takes the reviews too</span></code></pre>

  <div class="note">
    <span class="note-title">Nothing is copied by hand</span>
    <p>
      <code>gitwarren serve</code> binds <code>127.0.0.1</code> only and mints a
      token for that launch, printed in the URL it gives you. <code
        >gitwarren open</code
      > reads it and hands the whole URL to your browser, which swaps it for a
      cookie on the first request. A token is never persisted across a launch,
      and revoking it is quitting the process.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Keeping it running</h2>

  <p>
    <code>gitwarren serve</code> stops when you close the terminal. <code
      >gitwarren service install</code
    > is for wanting GitWarren there whenever you are: it registers a login item
    — a LaunchAgent on macOS, a <code>systemd --user</code> unit on Linux, an
    at-logon Scheduled Task on Windows — and starts it right away, so <code
      >gitwarren open</code
    > and the links an agent hands you always have something to open. <code
      >gitwarren service uninstall</code
    > stops it and removes the item.
  </p>

  <p>
    Whichever command you run first also writes two small scripts to{" "}
    <code>~/.gitwarren/bin/</code>: <code>gitwarren</code>, the stable path the
    app spawns this install by from another machine, and <code
      >gitwarren-mcp</code
    >, the one you point an agent at. On a headless host that is reached only
    from elsewhere, <code>gitwarren service install --no-login-item</code>{" "}
    writes them and registers nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>Updating it, and taking it off</h2>

  <p>
    <code>gitwarren update</code> moves this install to the newest release. It
    resolves the version, checks the download against the sha256 the release
    published in its own Homebrew formula, unpacks beside the destination and
    renames into place, has the <em>new</em> binary write the launchers — which
    is also the proof that it runs here — restarts the background GitWarren if
    one is running, and deletes the version it replaced.{" "}
    <code>gitwarren update --check</code> only says what is installed and what
    is newest. Neither happens on its own: nothing checks in the background, and
    the one request either makes is to GitHub, for the release.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is for an install GitWarren put there itself — the script above, or the
    app installing onto <a href="/docs/another-machine">another machine</a> over
    ssh. A Homebrew, npm or npx copy belongs to its package manager, and{" "}
    <code>update</code> names the command that is the right one rather than
    writing over files it does not own.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>gitwarren uninstall</code> goes the other way, and it takes more than
    the install: the login item, and the launchers in{" "}
    <code>~/.gitwarren/bin</code> that name this install. It prints the plan and
    asks before doing any of it. Your reviews are kept unless you add{" "}
    <code>--data</code> — they are the one thing here that cannot be downloaded
    again. A launcher belonging to a <em>different</em> GitWarren, commonly the
    desktop app, which writes <code>gitwarren-mcp</code> too, is named and left
    exactly where it is.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <span class="note-title">The failure with no symptom on this machine</span>
    <p>
      A launcher whose target is gone — after a <code>brew uninstall</code>,
      after an AppImage is deleted, after a checkout is rebuilt somewhere else —
      still sits there, and the only thing anyone sees is their agent reporting
      that the MCP server failed to connect: in another product, with nothing
      naming the cause. <code>gitwarren doctor</code> reads every path GitWarren
      asks another program to run, marks the broken ones and exits non-zero so a
      script can be what notices; <code>--fix</code> repoints a stale one. It
      never deletes — a file GitWarren did not write is reported and left alone.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Pointing an agent at it</h2>

  <p>
    If the agent is one that takes plugins, that is the shortest way in, and it
    brings more than the connection: the plugin also carries a note that teaches
    the agent when to open a review and how to answer the comments you leave on
    it.
  </p>

  <pre><code><span class="c"># Claude Code — add the marketplace, then install from it</span>
/plugin marketplace add klarluft/gitwarren-app
/plugin install gitwarren@gitwarren

<span class="c"># Gemini CLI</span>
gemini extensions install https://github.com/klarluft/gitwarren-app

<span class="c"># The note on its own, into any agent that reads skills</span>
npx skills add klarluft/gitwarren-app</code></pre>

  <p>
    Codex, Cursor, VS Code and Kiro read the same repository from their own
    plugin screens. The plugin is not an alternative to installing GitWarren
    here: with one already running on the machine it uses that one, so the links
    it hands back open in the app or the tab you already have. On a machine with
    nothing else on it, it fetches the published package and serves the review
    page itself for as long as the agent is running.
  </p>

  <p>
    For everything else &mdash; a harness with no plugin format, or a machine you
    would rather configure by hand &mdash; GitWarren ships an MCP server over
    stdio. Rather than a config snippet per harness, paste this into whatever
    agent runs on that machine:
  </p>

  <pre><code>Set up the GitWarren MCP server for yourself. It speaks MCP over
stdio and is started with the command ~/.gitwarren/bin/gitwarren-mcp
(no arguments, no environment). Register it under the name
"gitwarren" in your own MCP configuration, then call its
agent_identity tool to confirm it works.</code></pre>

  <p>
    Agents know their own configuration format better than a settings panel
    does; what they need from us is a stable command. <code
      >gitwarren agent-setup</code
    > prints the same sentence on any host, writes the launcher it names if it
    is not there yet, and <code>--manual</code> adds hand-editable snippets for
    the three config formats that exist.
  </p>

  <p>
    The MCP server is part of every install and reads the same SQLite file the
    browser view does, so an agent can open and comment on reviews whether or
    not GitWarren is being served. What serving adds is that the link an agent
    hands back opens in a browser.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every machine you add runs its own MCP server, local to itself, reading real
    paths on its own disk. An agent never talks to GitWarren over a network.
  </p>

  <p>
    Next: <a href={DOCS_INDEX}>all the guides</a>, or carry on below.
  </p>
